---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SITE } from "../../config";

const title = "Nanoplasty vs. Keratin Treatment: Differences, Cost & Which to Choose";
const description =
  "Keratin treatment vs. nanoplasty: chemistry, downtime, longevity, and results compared, so you can choose the right formaldehyde-free-friendly option.";
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} path="/keratin-vs-nanoplasty/">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10 prose-section">
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-600 font-medium mb-2">
      <a href="/" class="hover:underline">Keratin Treatment Elmwood Park</a> / Keratin vs. Nanoplasty
    </p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-serif font-bold text-brand-900 mb-5">
      Keratin Treatment vs. Nanoplasty
    </h1>
    <p>
      Both smooth frizz and add shine, and both can be part of a formaldehyde-free routine — but they get there
      differently, and the results aren't identical. Here's how traditional keratin and nanoplasty actually compare.
      For a full deep-dive on nanoplasty specifically, see our
      <a href="/nanoplasty-hair-treatment/" class="text-brand-600 font-medium">complete nanoplasty guide →</a>.
    </p>

    <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
      <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="bg-brand-50">
            <th class="p-3 border border-stone-200 font-semibold text-brand-900">Factor</th>
            <th class="p-3 border border-stone-200 font-semibold text-brand-900">Traditional Keratin</th>
            <th class="p-3 border border-stone-200 font-semibold text-brand-900">Nanoplasty</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-stone-700">
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 border border-stone-200 font-medium">Formaldehyde</td>
            <td class="p-3 border border-stone-200">Often present in classic formulas</td>
            <td class="p-3 border border-stone-200">Formaldehyde-free by design</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 border border-stone-200 font-medium">Frizz reduction</td>
            <td class="p-3 border border-stone-200">Maximum, most dramatic</td>
            <td class="p-3 border border-stone-200">Strong, slightly more subtle</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 border border-stone-200 font-medium">Curl retention</td>
            <td class="p-3 border border-stone-200">Less — more relaxing effect</td>
            <td class="p-3 border border-stone-200">More natural texture retained</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 border border-stone-200 font-medium">Downtime before washing</td>
            <td class="p-3 border border-stone-200">2–3 days</td>
            <td class="p-3 border border-stone-200">Varies by formula, often shorter</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 border border-stone-200 font-medium">Typical longevity</td>
            <td class="p-3 border border-stone-200">3–5 months</td>
            <td class="p-3 border border-stone-200">2–4 months</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>How They Work, Chemically</h2>
    <p>
      Traditional keratin smoothing coats the hair shaft with keratin protein and seals it in with a flat iron;
      classic versions of this process often use formaldehyde or a formaldehyde-releasing ingredient to make that
      bond stronger and longer-lasting. Nanoplasty takes a different approach, using ingredients engineered at a
      much smaller molecular size so they can penetrate the hair shaft and smooth it from within, without needing
      formaldehyde chemistry to do it. For more on formaldehyde-free chemistry generally, see our
      <a href="/formaldehyde-free-keratin/" class="text-brand-600 font-medium">formaldehyde-free keratin guide →</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Results: Smoothing Intensity and Texture</h2>
    <p>
      Traditional keratin tends to deliver the most dramatic, straightest result, and it relaxes curl pattern more
      than nanoplasty does. Nanoplasty is designed to smooth and reduce frizz while leaving more of your hair's
      natural movement and body intact — many clients describe it as leaving hair feeling more "like itself," just
      calmer. If you have looser waves or curls and want to keep some texture, nanoplasty is usually the closer fit.
      If your hair is very thick, coarse, or extremely frizzy and you want maximum control, traditional keratin
      often gets you further.
    </p>

    <h2>Longevity and Upkeep</h2>
    <p>
      Traditional keratin generally lasts a bit longer than nanoplasty, largely because of the stronger bond
      formaldehyde-based chemistry creates. Both fade faster with sulfate shampoos and heavy washing, and both
      benefit from the same core aftercare — see our
      <a href="/keratin-aftercare/" class="text-brand-600 font-medium">keratin aftercare guide →</a> for specifics
      that apply to either treatment.
    </p>

    <h2>Which One Should You Choose?</h2>
    <p>
      Choose <strong>traditional keratin</strong> if frizz control and longevity are your top priorities and you're
      comfortable with (or specifically want) a formula that includes formaldehyde. Choose <strong>nanoplasty</strong>
      if avoiding formaldehyde is a priority, or if you want to keep more natural texture and volume while still
      cutting down on frizz. Availability varies by stylist since nanoplasty is a newer service — request a quote and
      we'll confirm what's offered for your appointment.
    </p>
    <p>
      Still deciding? See the full
      <a href="/" class="text-brand-600 font-medium">keratin treatment overview →</a> or compare against a Brazilian
      blowout in our <a href="/keratin-vs-brazilian-blowout/" class="text-brand-600 font-medium">keratin vs. Brazilian blowout guide →</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Questions about which fits your hair? <a href={`tel:${SITE.phone}`} class="text-brand-600 font-medium">Call {SITE.phoneDisplay}</a>
      and a stylist will help you decide.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
